  "details": "EDIT: This has already been done! I just didn't notice because they were made into separate strings entirely and the old outdated ones weren't deleted.\n\nI'm noticing that the string \"No {0} - You're all caught up!\" for indicating that you have none of a certain notification type is an issue when translating it to French, and I'm almost certain it's gonna be issue in other languages. (https://crowdin.com/translate/vrchat-application/19/en-fr#25013)\n\nNot only does it make conjugating confusing because it assumes the \"No\" phrase is conjugated the same across all the notification types, it also relies heavily on English's sentence structure. I'm sure there's a language out there that needs to incorporate the \"No\" into the notification types in a more complex manner than just being before or after.\n\nPerhaps separate these \"No {0}\" strings into:\n\n\"No Invites\"\n\n\"No Invite Requests\"\n\netc.\n\nand keep \"You're all caught up!\" as its own string?\n\nI believe that could solve this problem universally as they're two separate exclamations separated by \"-\" that don't interact.",
